WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.769
Represent sparse contest graphs with a list of neighbor lists indexed by vertex number.

00:00:05.769 --> 00:00:11.273
Adjacency matrices waste quadratic memory when vertices reach one hundred thousand.

00:00:11.273 --> 00:00:17.440
Always add both endpoints for undirected edges and only the forward arrow for directed edges.

00:00:17.708 --> 00:00:24.593
Breadth-first search uses a deque to visit vertices in increasing order of edge distance from the source.

00:00:24.593 --> 00:00:32.462
Mark a vertex visited at the moment you push it into the queue, not when you pop it, to prevent duplicate queue entries.

00:00:32.462 --> 00:00:36.068
BFS computes exact shortest paths in unweighted graphs.

00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:44.991
Depth-first search plunges along a path to discover connected components, cycles, and bipartite two-colorings.

00:00:44.991 --> 00:00:53.176
In Python, use an explicit list as a stack instead of deep recursion so skewed chain graphs never crash.

00:00:53.176 --> 00:00:58.213
Alternate colors zero and one across edges to detect odd cycles.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:06.900
When distances are measured from the nearest of many starting points, seed all sources into the queue at distance zero before starting BFS.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:16.751
If edge weights are only zero or one, push zero-weight transitions to the front of the deque and one-weight transitions to the back for linear-time shortest paths.
